1
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,900
[thunder rumbling]

2
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:11,050
<i>Captain Marlowe, the storm is getting worse.</i>

3
00:00:11,650 --> 00:00:15,850
Then bring the Calypso about, Mr. Hendricks.

4
00:00:16,450 --> 00:00:20,950
We're not going to make it to Port Avalon by dawn.

5
00:00:21,550 --> 00:00:24,950
I don't care about the dawn.

6
00:00:25,550 --> 00:00:28,650
I care about the crew.

7
00:00:29,250 --> 00:00:33,100
Sir, the barometer is still dropping.

8
00:00:33,700 --> 00:00:38,150
Ladies and gentlemen, I need everyone below deck!

9
00:00:38,750 --> 00:00:41,900
Where is Dr. Whitcombe?

10
00:00:42,500 --> 00:00:46,250
He's in the infirmary with the boy.

11
00:00:46,850 --> 00:00:50,300
The boy has a fever, Captain.

12
00:00:50,900 --> 00:00:54,100
Then he'll have to wait.

13
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:56,850
♪ ♪

14
00:00:57,450 --> 00:01:00,500
Hold on to something!

15
00:01:01,100 --> 00:01:03,850
(wave crashing)

16
00:01:04,450 --> 00:01:07,800
Hendricks, can you hear me?

17
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:12,150
Aye, Captain. The rudder is jammed.

18
00:01:12,750 --> 00:01:15,650
{\an8}Jammed? How?

19
00:01:16,250 --> 00:01:20,150
A rope from the mizzen mast fouled it.

20
00:01:20,750 --> 00:01:24,1000
Get Dalton and Pierce down there with an axe.

21
00:01:25,600 --> 00:01:28,700
They're already on it.

22
00:01:29,300 --> 00:01:32,950
You know, in thirty years at sea,

23
00:01:33,550 --> 00:01:37,250
I have never seen a sky like that.

24
00:01:37,850 --> 00:01:40,850
Neither have I, sir.

25
00:01:41,450 --> 00:01:45,900
Do you believe the old stories about the Calypso?

26
00:01:46,500 --> 00:01:49,150
What stories?

27
00:01:49,750 --> 00:01:54,850
That she carries the ghost of her first captain, Elias Thorne.

28
00:01:55,450 --> 00:01:58,750
Nonsense. Sailors' gossip.

29
00:01:59,350 --> 00:02:03,150
Then who's that standing at the bow?

30
00:02:03,750 --> 00:02:07,050
Where? I don't see anyone.

31
00:02:07,650 --> 00:02:10,750
Look again, Hendricks.

32
00:02:11,350 --> 00:02:14,500
It's just the fog, sir.

33
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:17,950
Maybe. Maybe not.

34
00:02:18,550 --> 00:02:22,150
Light the lanterns, all of them.

35
00:02:22,750 --> 00:02:26,150
The rudder is free, Captain!

36
00:02:26,750 --> 00:02:30,400
Good work. Steer north-northeast.

37
00:02:30,1000 --> 00:02:34,850
Toward the lighthouse at Cape Sorrow?

38
00:02:35,450 --> 00:02:39,700
It's the only safe harbor within fifty miles.

39
00:02:40,300 --> 00:02:43,350
Then God help us all.

40
00:02:43,950 --> 00:02:48,350
God has nothing to do with it. Hold your course!
